AB  - Deep learning applications in crop protection are impeded by the scarcity and high redundancy of existing datasets, which hinders models from learning discriminative representations for fine-grained diagnosis. While generative AI offers a potential solution, existing methods struggle to trade-off between pathological fidelity and diversity, as domain-specific models often lack variety while generic models suffer from feature hallucinations. To address this, we propose the collaborative diffusion-VLM framework (CDVF), a novel paradigm that synergizes distinct generative engines to synthesize high-value cotton pest and disease imagery. Specifically, our framework implements a two-stage strategy: (1) Distribution internalization: As the primary engine, a stable diffusion model tailored with category-specific low-rank adaptation is employed to faithfully reconstruct distinctive lesion textures, effectively scaling up sparse dataset. (2) Distribution extrapolation: To further transcend the visual homogeneity of seed samples, we leverage a vision-language model (VLM) via in-context learning. By unlocking its pre-trained world knowledge, the VLM injects diverse open-world contexts (e.g., complex backgrounds and varying illumination) absent in the source domain. Furthermore, we construct CoD14-plus, the most categorically diverse benchmark to date. Experiments demonstrate that expanding the dataset approximately 4 × yielded a 13.19% accuracy improvement in downstream classification, significantly outperforming the traditional augmentation strategy, which yielded a 6.91% gain. This validates our approach as a scalable solution for overcoming data bottlenecks in intelligent agriculture.
