AB  - Helium (He) recovery from methane (CH4)-rich natural gas is essential for securing He resources but remains challenging because weakly adsorbed He must be separated from strongly adsorbed CH4 in porous membranes. Although metal-organic framework (MOF) membranes offer ordered and tunable channels, most reported polycrystalline MOF membranes still exhibit limited He/CH4 selectivity, highlighting the need for membranes with more precisely regulated diffusion pathways. Herein, we report a crown-ether-confined aluminum MOF membrane in which benzo-15-crown-5 ether (C15-5) is in situ incorporated into MIL-160 cavities to create methane-blocking diffusion gates. The confined crown ether locally narrows the effective diffusion pathways and selectively increases CH4 transport resistance, thereby strengthening diffusion-controlled He/CH4 molecular sieving. The optimized C30@MIL-160 membrane delivered a mixed-gas He permeance of 227.4 GPU and a He/CH4 selectivity of 83.6 at 298 K and 1 bar, far higher than pristine MIL-160 and superior to most reported MOF and zeolite membranes. The membrane enabled 76-fold He enrichment from a 0.1 vol% He feed and remained stable over nearly 100 h under operational disturbances. Combined experimental and simulation results confirm that the enhanced separation originates from crown-ether-amplified He/CH4 diffusion differences, and preliminary process assessment highlights its potential for energy- and cost-efficient helium recovery.
