AB  - Stainless steel is widely valued for its stability, corrosion resistance, affordability, and processability, making it a staple for components like valves, bearings, and gears. However, strong acidic environments often cause corrosion with cracks, pitting, and by-products on the surfaces of stainless steel and Ni-Cr-based alloy, losing the reliability of components. The corroded parts raise concerns of user safety and the productivity of industries. This study coated polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) thin film on stainless steel via sputtering and characterized film properties by scanning electron microscopy, atomic force microscopy, water contact angle, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The corrosion resistance of PTFE films coated on stainless steel was evaluated via electrochemical impedance spectroscopy in a hydrochloric acid (HCl) environment. Chemically stable PTFE thin film proved higher corrosion resistance compared to uncoated stainless steel, demonstrating that PTFE thin film is effective for mitigating metal substrate corrosion. To review the applicability of sputtered PTFE thin films to commercial valves for industrial applications, mechanical properties, including adhesion, wear, hardness, and elastic modulus were studied, revealing superior values. Furthermore, the PTFE coating was applied to the diaphragm valve and operated without leakage of acidic medium. It performed well in the operation test, supplying HCl in a vacuum environment. After 10,000 cycles of repeated bending of the diaphragm, PTFE-coated diaphragm displayed crack-free surfaces, while the bare diaphragm had numerous cracks on its surfaces with the presence of chlorine in XPS analysis.
